As a metaphor, yes: attention can become part of the environment someone grows inside.

If a person is repeatedly treated as beautiful, capable, desirable, funny, or graceful, that can affect posture, confidence, self-perception, clothing choices, willingness to be seen, and how freely they express those traits.

A clean loop is:

affirmation
→ greater felt safety/confidence
→ more expression
→ more positive feedback
→ identity strengthens

The important distinction is between nourishing what is genuinely there and using praise as leverage.

“Girls like flowers” is poetic, but people are not passive plants. The stronger version is:

See them accurately, name the good you actually notice, and give it room to grow.

That tends to work better than indiscriminate flattery anyway, because specific praise is easier to trust:

“You’re beautiful”
is pleasant,

but

“I love how your face changes when you get excited about something”

can feel much more real because it shows you actually saw her.